/* Added curriculum for the AI Consultant Track: lessons that fill interview gaps, a new Module 14,
   plus their notes sections and flashcards. Generated from a source file; loads before the main app script. */
window.EXTRAS={"lessons":[{"mid":"m1","lesson":{"id":"l9","t":"The four kinds of caching","min":7,"html":"<p class=\"lead\">'Caching' means four different things in an AI system, and mixing them up in an interview is an instant credibility leak. Each one lives in a different place, saves something different, and carries a different risk.</p><h3>1 · KV cache: inside one request</h3><p>While generating, the model stores the Keys and Values of every token so far, so each new token doesn't recompute the past. It's automatic, it lives inside a single request, and it's the reason decode is memory-bound. You never 'propose' a KV cache as a fix; you explain it.</p><h3>2 · Prompt (prefix) caching: across requests, at the provider</h3><p>The provider stores the processed state of an identical prompt prefix and reuses it on the next request that starts with exactly the same bytes. Cached reads are roughly 90% cheaper and the first token arrives sooner. Crucially, the model still writes a fresh answer every time: only the reading of the fixed part is skipped.</p><ul><li>Put stable content first (system prompt, tools, examples, long documents), variable content last (history, the user's question)</li><li>One changed character near the top (a timestamp, a user name) breaks the cache for everything after it</li><li>Caches expire after a short idle period, so they pay off on steady traffic</li></ul><h3>3 · Response cache: exact match, in your system</h3><p>Your application stores the full answer keyed on the exact input (a hash of the prompt) and returns it without calling the model at all. Safe, but it only hits when the input is identical.</p><h3>4 · Semantic cache: similar match, in your system</h3><p>Embed the incoming question; if a previous question is similar enough, return its stored answer. It's the biggest saving and the biggest risk.</p><div class=\"box warnbx\"><div class=\"bx-h\">Watch out</div>'Can I return a laptop?' and 'Can I return a phone?' are near neighbours in embedding space but may have different answers. A loose threshold returns a confident wrong answer.</div><ul><li>Use it only for stable, non-personal answers (FAQs, policy explanations), with a strict similarity threshold</li><li>Never for live or personal data ('where is my order', 'what is my balance')</li><li>Invalidate on a time limit (TTL) and whenever source documents change (version the cache keys)</li></ul><div class=\"tblwrap\"><table class=\"tbl\"><tr><th>Cache</th><th>Where it lives</th><th>What it saves</th><th>Main risk</th></tr><tr><td>KV cache</td><td>Inside one request</td><td>Recomputing past tokens</td><td>None: it's how inference works</td></tr><tr><td>Prompt cache</td><td>Provider, across requests</td><td>Re-reading a fixed prefix</td><td>Silent misses if the prefix changes</td></tr><tr><td>Response cache</td><td>Your system</td><td>The whole model call (exact input)</td><td>Stale answers</td></tr><tr><td>Semantic cache</td><td>Your system</td><td>The whole call (similar input)</td><td>Wrong answers to near-miss questions</td></tr></table></div><div class=\"box key\"><div class=\"bx-h\">Say it in the room</div>'KV cache is inside one call; prompt caching reuses an identical prefix across calls; response and semantic caches skip the model by reusing a stored answer, and the semantic one needs a strict threshold.'</div>","checks":[{"q":"A support bot's prompt starts with 'Today is <date and time>' followed by a 4,000-token policy document. The cache-hit rate is near zero. Why?","opts":["The changing timestamp at the top breaks the exact-prefix match","Prompt caching only works for prompts under 1,000 tokens","The provider cannot cache documents, only system instructions","The KV cache is full, so the provider stops caching new prompts"],"a":0,"why":"Prompt caching needs a byte-identical prefix. A timestamp at the very top changes every request, so nothing after it can be reused. Move dynamic content to the end."},{"q":"Where is a semantic cache the riskiest choice?","opts":["Answering 'what are your opening hours?'","Answering 'where is my parcel right now?'","Explaining the general returns policy","Describing how to reset a password"],"a":1,"why":"A semantic cache returns a stored answer for a similar question. Live, personal data changes per user and per minute, so a reused answer would be wrong."}],"added":true}},{"mid":"m1","lesson":{"id":"l10","t":"The prompt engineering toolkit","min":7,"html":"<p class=\"lead\">Prompting is now an engineering discipline: prompts are versioned, tested against evals, and changed one variable at a time. These are the techniques worth naming, and when each one earns its place.</p><h3>The core techniques</h3><ul><li><strong>Zero-shot</strong>: clear instructions, no examples. Start here</li><li><strong>Few-shot</strong>: 2 to 5 worked examples. Selection beats count: pick examples that cover the tricky cases</li><li><strong>Role and audience</strong>: say who the output is for and what it's used for, not 'you are a world-class expert'</li><li><strong>Structure the prompt</strong>: separate instructions, context and the question with clear sections or tags</li><li><strong>Output format</strong>: specify the schema, or better, use structured outputs</li></ul><h3>Reasoning techniques</h3><ul><li><strong>Chain-of-thought (CoT)</strong>: ask for step-by-step reasoning on non-reasoning models. Redundant on reasoning models</li><li><strong>Self-consistency</strong>: sample several answers and take the majority, for higher accuracy at higher cost</li><li><strong>Decomposition / prompt chaining</strong>: split a big task into small testable calls (extract → classify → draft)</li><li><strong>ReAct</strong>: interleave reasoning and tool calls in an agent loop</li><li><strong>Self-critique / reflection</strong>: draft, critique against criteria, revise</li></ul><h3>Grounding techniques</h3><ul><li>Put the source material in the prompt and instruct: answer only from it, cite it, say 'I don't know' otherwise</li><li>Put critical instructions at the start and end of long prompts (lost-in-the-middle)</li></ul><h3>Prompt hygiene</h3><ul><li>Version prompts in git, test every change against the golden set</li><li>Change one thing at a time so you know what helped</li><li>Keep stable text at the top for prompt caching</li></ul><div class=\"box warnbx\"><div class=\"bx-h\">Watch out</div>Dropped folklore: threats, bribes, 'take a deep breath', long persona padding, and manual chain-of-thought on reasoning models.</div><div class=\"box key\"><div class=\"bx-h\">Say it in the room</div>'I start zero-shot with clear structure, add a few well-chosen examples if needed, decompose complex tasks into chained calls, and only keep a change if the eval set says it helped.'</div>","checks":[{"q":"A team adds 'think step by step' to every prompt sent to a reasoning model. What's the best advice?","opts":["Keep it, because it always improves accuracy on any model","Replace it with a longer persona describing a world-class expert","Remove it, since reasoning models already think and it can hurt","Move it to the end of the prompt so caching still works"],"a":2,"why":"Reasoning models generate their own thinking tokens. Manual chain-of-thought instructions are redundant there and can degrade results."},{"q":"Which technique trades extra cost for higher accuracy by sampling several answers?","opts":["Few-shot prompting","Prompt chaining","Structured outputs","Self-consistency"],"a":3,"why":"Self-consistency samples multiple reasoning paths and takes the majority answer, improving accuracy at a multiple of the cost."}],"added":true}},{"mid":"m2","lesson":{"id":"l6","t":"MLOps for classical models","min":7,"html":"<p class=\"lead\">A churn or fraud model is not done when it scores well in a notebook. Classical ML has its own production discipline, and interviewers for data and AI roles probe it.</p><h3>The lifecycle</h3><ol><li>Data pipeline produces features on a schedule</li><li>Train, validate, register the model with its metrics and data version</li><li>Deploy: batch scoring (nightly scores to a table) or real-time scoring (an API)</li><li>Monitor inputs, predictions and outcomes</li><li>Retrain on a schedule or when monitoring says so</li></ol><h3>The building blocks</h3><ul><li><strong>Feature store</strong>: one place where features are defined once and served the same way for training and for live scoring, avoiding training-serving skew</li><li><strong>Model registry</strong>: every model version with its training data, parameters, metrics and approval status</li><li><strong>Experiment tracking</strong>: every run's settings and results, so you can reproduce the winner (MLflow is the common name)</li><li><strong>Batch vs real-time</strong>: most business models (churn, propensity) are batch; fraud and pricing often need real-time</li></ul><h3>What goes wrong in production</h3><ul><li><strong>Data drift</strong>: the inputs change (new customer mix, new product)</li><li><strong>Concept drift</strong>: the relationship changes (what predicted churn last year no longer does)</li><li><strong>Training-serving skew</strong>: features computed differently live than in training</li><li><strong>Label delay</strong>: you only learn whether a prediction was right weeks later, so monitor inputs and prediction distributions in the meantime</li><li><strong>Feedback loops</strong>: the model's own actions change future data (a retention offer stops the churn you were predicting)</li></ul><div class=\"box key\"><div class=\"bx-h\">Say it in the room</div>'I'd register the model with its data version, monitor input and prediction drift because true labels arrive late, and retrain on a schedule or when drift crosses a threshold.'</div>","checks":[{"q":"A fraud model's accuracy slowly falls although no code changed. Fraudsters have adopted new tactics. This is:","opts":["Concept drift","Training-serving skew","Label leakage","Underfitting"],"a":0,"why":"Concept drift: the relationship between inputs and the outcome has changed, so the patterns the model learned no longer hold."},{"q":"What is a feature store mainly for?","opts":["Storing the trained model files and their versions","Serving features identically for training and live scoring","Tracking the hyperparameters of every experiment run","Keeping the raw data lake organised by business owner"],"a":1,"why":"A feature store defines each feature once and serves it the same way in training and production, preventing training-serving skew."}],"added":true}},{"mid":"m3","lesson":{"id":"l3","t":"Text-to-SQL: talking to your data","min":8,"html":"<p class=\"lead\">'Ask your data in plain English' is one of the most requested enterprise AI use cases, and one of the easiest to get confidently wrong. The hard part isn't writing SQL; it's meaning.</p><h3>The reference flow</h3><ol><li>The user asks a question</li><li><strong>Retrieve the relevant schema</strong>: the few tables and columns that matter, with descriptions, not the whole warehouse</li><li>The LLM writes SQL, guided by example question-to-SQL pairs</li><li><strong>Validate before running</strong>: it parses, it's read-only, it touches only allowed tables, it has a LIMIT, and a dry run shows acceptable cost</li><li>Execute</li><li>Answer in words, and <strong>show the SQL and the numbers</strong> so the user can check</li></ol><h3>Why it fails</h3><ul><li>Ambiguous metrics: 'revenue' could be gross or net, booked or recognised, in which currency</li><li>Wrong joins that silently duplicate rows and inflate totals</li><li>Missing business filters: test accounts, cancelled orders, internal users</li><li>Expensive queries that scan a whole warehouse</li></ul><h3>The fixes</h3><ul><li>A <strong>semantic layer</strong>: one agreed definition per metric that the model uses instead of guessing</li><li>Good column descriptions and a library of verified example queries</li><li>Ask a <strong>clarifying question</strong> when the request is ambiguous</li><li>A <strong>read-only</strong> service account with row-level security, so it can never see more than the user may</li><li>Cost guards: dry runs, byte limits, timeouts</li></ul><h3>How to evaluate it</h3><p>Use <strong>execution accuracy</strong>: run the generated query and compare its result with the expected result on a golden set of real business questions. Comparing SQL text is useless, because many different queries are correct.</p><div class=\"box key\"><div class=\"bx-h\">Say it in the room</div>'The hard part of text-to-SQL is agreeing what revenue means, so I'd put a semantic layer underneath, validate every query before it runs, and evaluate on execution accuracy.'</div>","checks":[{"q":"Two generated queries are written differently but return the same correct result. How should a text-to-SQL eval treat them?","opts":["Fail the one that differs most from the reference SQL text","Fail both until the SQL text matches the reference exactly","Pass both, because execution accuracy compares results","Pass only the shorter one, since it is cheaper to run"],"a":2,"why":"Execution accuracy compares results, not SQL strings. Many different queries are correct."},{"q":"Finance and marketing get different 'revenue' answers from the same text-to-SQL bot. The root fix is:","opts":["A larger model that writes more careful SQL","More example queries for the marketing team","Longer column names in the warehouse schema","A semantic layer with one agreed metric definition"],"a":3,"why":"The disagreement is about meaning, not SQL skill. A semantic layer gives the model one agreed definition to use."}],"added":true}},{"mid":"m4","lesson":{"id":"l3","t":"Chunking strategies in depth","min":9,"html":"<p class=\"lead\">The chunk is the unit you retrieve: retrieval can never return half a chunk. That makes chunking the decision that sets the ceiling on RAG quality, and interviewers love asking which strategies you know and when you'd use each.</p><h3>The trade-off</h3><p>Chunks that are too big blur several topics into one vector and add noise and cost. Chunks that are too small lose their context: a chunk saying 'the window is 14 days' is useless if it doesn't say for which product.</p><h3>The strategies, simple to advanced</h3><ol><li><strong>Fixed-size</strong>: every N tokens with overlap. Simple and predictable, but cuts sentences in half</li><li><strong>Recursive</strong> (the default): split on paragraphs, then sentences, then words, until each piece is under the limit</li><li><strong>Sentence or paragraph</strong>: natural boundaries, uneven sizes</li><li><strong>Structure-aware</strong>: split on headings, sections, slides, list items or code functions. Best for manuals, policies and code</li><li><strong>Semantic</strong>: embed sentences and cut where the meaning shifts. Costlier; gains vary by corpus, so test it</li><li><strong>Parent-child (small-to-big)</strong>: retrieve small, precise chunks, but send the whole parent section to the model</li><li><strong>Sentence window</strong>: retrieve one matching sentence and add its neighbours</li><li><strong>Contextual chunking</strong>: prepend a short LLM-written line to each chunk saying where it sits ('From the 2026 refund policy, electronics section'). Reported to cut retrieval failures substantially</li><li><strong>Proposition chunking</strong>: an LLM rewrites text into small standalone facts. Very precise, expensive</li><li><strong>Late chunking</strong>: embed the whole document with a long-context embedding model first, then split the embeddings, so each chunk keeps document-wide context</li></ol><h3>The knobs</h3><ul><li>Size around 200 to 1,000 tokens (default 400 to 600), within the embedding model's input limit</li><li>Overlap of 10 to 20%</li><li>Metadata on every chunk: source, title, heading path, date, language, permissions</li></ul><h3>Awkward content</h3><ul><li><strong>Tables</strong>: keep whole, or one chunk per row with the column headers repeated</li><li><strong>PDFs</strong>: parsing quality comes first (layout, OCR, headers and footers)</li><li><strong>FAQs</strong>: one question and its answer per chunk</li><li><strong>Code</strong>: split by function or class</li></ul><h3>Symptoms and fixes</h3><div class=\"tblwrap\"><table class=\"tbl\"><tr><th>Symptom</th><th>Likely fix</th></tr><tr><td>Answer split across two chunks</td><td>More overlap, or parent-child</td></tr><tr><td>Chunk makes no sense alone</td><td>Heading breadcrumbs or contextual chunking</td></tr><tr><td>Tables come back garbled</td><td>Structure-aware parsing</td></tr><tr><td>Right document, wrong section</td><td>Smaller chunks plus reranking</td></tr></table></div><div class=\"box key\"><div class=\"bx-h\">Say it in the room</div>'I'd start with recursive chunking around 500 tokens with overlap and heading metadata, then test structure-aware or parent-child chunking against recall@K on the golden set.'</div>","checks":[{"q":"Retrieved chunks contain the right numbers but the model can't tell which product they refer to. Best first fix:","opts":["Add heading breadcrumbs or contextual text to each chunk","Switch to a larger embedding model","Reduce the chunk size to a single sentence","Increase top-K from 5 to 50 chunks"],"a":0,"why":"The chunk lost its context. Adding the heading path or a short contextual line restores what the fragment is about."},{"q":"Which strategy retrieves small precise chunks but sends the larger section to the model?","opts":["Semantic chunking","Parent-child (small-to-big)","Fixed-size chunking","Proposition chunking"],"a":1,"why":"Parent-child retrieval matches on small chunks for precision, then passes the full parent section for context."}],"added":true}},{"mid":"m4","lesson":{"id":"l4","t":"Vector maths, indexes & retrieval metrics","min":8,"html":"<p class=\"lead\">The numbers behind retrieval: how big an index gets, which index types exist, and how to measure whether retrieval works. These come up as quick calculations and 'what would you measure' questions.</p><h3>Embedding dimensions</h3><p>Common sizes are 384, 768, 1,024, 1,536 and 3,072. More dimensions capture more nuance, but cost more storage and slow search. <strong>Matryoshka</strong> embeddings are trained so they can be shortened with little quality loss, a cheap way to trade accuracy for cost.</p><h3>Storage maths</h3><p>Size = number of vectors × dimensions × 4 bytes (float32). One million vectors at 1,536 dimensions is 1,000,000 × 1,536 × 4 ≈ <strong>6 GB</strong>, before index overhead. <strong>Quantisation</strong> cuts it: float16 halves it, int8 quarters it, binary shrinks it 32×, each with some recall loss.</p><h3>Index types</h3><ul><li><strong>Flat</strong>: exact search against every vector. Fine for small sets</li><li><strong>IVF</strong>: clusters the vectors and only searches the clusters nearest the query</li><li><strong>HNSW</strong>: a layered graph, fast with high recall, but memory-hungry. The default</li><li><strong>PQ</strong> (product quantisation): compresses vectors to save memory</li></ul><h3>Getting the right top-K</h3><ul><li><strong>Metadata filters</strong> (date, product, permissions): pre-filtering is accurate; post-filtering can leave too few results</li><li>Retrieve 20 to 50, rerank down to about 5</li><li><strong>MMR</strong> (maximal marginal relevance) diversifies results so you don't get five near-duplicates</li></ul><h3>Encoders</h3><ul><li><strong>Bi-encoder</strong>: query and document embedded separately. Fast, used for retrieval</li><li><strong>Cross-encoder</strong>: reads both together. Accurate, used for reranking</li><li><strong>ColBERT</strong>: compares word-level vectors, a middle ground</li></ul><h3>Retrieval metrics</h3><div class=\"tblwrap\"><table class=\"tbl\"><tr><th>Metric</th><th>Question it answers</th></tr><tr><td>recall@K</td><td>Was the right chunk anywhere in the top K?</td></tr><tr><td>precision@K</td><td>How much of the top K was relevant?</td></tr><tr><td>MRR</td><td>How high was the first correct result?</td></tr><tr><td>nDCG</td><td>Were the most relevant results ranked highest?</td></tr></table></div><div class=\"box key\"><div class=\"bx-h\">Say it in the room</div>'A million 1,536-dimension vectors is about 6 GB in float32, so at scale I'd consider quantisation or shorter embeddings, and I'd judge retrieval on recall@K before anything else.'</div>","checks":[{"q":"Roughly how much memory do 2 million float32 vectors of 768 dimensions need, before index overhead?","opts":["About 600 MB","About 60 GB","About 6 GB","About 1.5 GB"],"a":2,"why":"2,000,000 × 768 × 4 bytes ≈ 6.1 GB."},{"q":"Which metric best captures whether the correct chunk appeared at all among the retrieved results?","opts":["nDCG","Precision@K","BLEU","Recall@K"],"a":3,"why":"Recall@K asks whether the relevant item was anywhere in the top K, the first thing to check in RAG."}],"added":true}},{"mid":"m5","lesson":{"id":"l5","t":"How models are trained & tuned","min":7,"html":"<p class=\"lead\">You don't train frontier models as a consultant, but you must explain the pipeline in one breath and know which tuning option fits a client's problem.</p><h3>The training pipeline</h3><ol><li><strong>Pre-training</strong>: predict the next token over trillions of tokens. The model gains knowledge and language, not helpful behaviour</li><li><strong>Supervised fine-tuning (SFT)</strong>: train on example prompts paired with ideal answers, so it follows instructions</li><li><strong>Preference tuning</strong>: teach it which answers people prefer</li></ol><ul><li><strong>RLHF</strong>: train a reward model on human rankings, then optimise the LLM against it</li><li><strong>DPO</strong>: learn directly from pairs of better and worse answers, simpler and now common</li></ul><h3>Cheaper ways to tune</h3><ul><li><strong>LoRA</strong>: train small add-on matrices instead of all the weights. Cheap, fast, swappable</li><li><strong>QLoRA</strong>: LoRA on a quantised base model, so large models can be tuned on modest GPUs</li><li><strong>Distillation</strong>: a big model generates training data or targets that teach a small model one narrow task</li></ul><h3>Quantisation</h3><p>Storing weights in fewer bits (16 → 8 → 4) cuts memory and speeds inference with a small quality loss. It's how big open models run on a single GPU or a laptop.</p><h3>Risks to name</h3><ul><li><strong>Catastrophic forgetting</strong>: narrow tuning degrades general skills</li><li>Overfitting to a small dataset</li><li>Lock-in: every base-model upgrade means re-tuning and re-evaluating</li></ul><div class=\"box key\"><div class=\"bx-h\">Say it in the room</div>'Pre-training gives knowledge, SFT teaches instruction-following, and preference tuning such as RLHF or DPO teaches which answers people prefer. For a client, LoRA is usually the right tuning option.'</div>","checks":[{"q":"Which step teaches a model to prefer one answer over another using ranked pairs, without a separate reward model?","opts":["DPO","Pre-training","SFT","Quantisation"],"a":0,"why":"DPO (direct preference optimisation) learns from better-vs-worse pairs directly, without training a reward model as RLHF does."},{"q":"A client wants to tune a 70B open model but only has a single mid-range GPU. Best option:","opts":["Full fine-tuning of all weights","QLoRA on a quantised base model","Pre-training a smaller model from scratch","RLHF with a new reward model"],"a":1,"why":"QLoRA trains small adapters on a 4-bit quantised base model, fitting large models on modest hardware."}],"added":true}},{"mid":"m6","lesson":{"id":"l2","t":"Context windows & agent memory","min":7,"html":"<p class=\"lead\">Agents run long, accumulate history, and need to remember across sessions. How you manage what's in the context window, and what's saved outside it, decides cost, quality and privacy.</p><h3>The context window is a budget</h3><p>Everything the model sees in one call counts: system prompt, tool definitions, conversation history, retrieved documents and the output itself. Bigger windows still cost more per call and still suffer lost-in-the-middle, so treat context as a budget to allocate, not a bucket to fill.</p><h3>Managing long conversations</h3><ul><li><strong>Sliding window</strong>: keep only the last N turns</li><li><strong>Summarisation</strong>: replace older turns with a running summary</li><li><strong>Compaction</strong>: when history gets long, rewrite it shorter while keeping decisions and facts</li><li>Keep stable content at the top so prompt caching still hits</li></ul><h3>Types of memory</h3><ul><li><strong>Short-term (working)</strong>: the current task and its trace</li><li><strong>Long-term (episodic and semantic)</strong>: past interactions and facts saved to a store and retrieved when relevant</li><li><strong>Profile</strong>: stable facts about the user or account</li><li><strong>Procedural</strong>: learned instructions about how to do a task</li></ul><h3>Design questions to ask</h3><ul><li>Who decides what gets saved: the model, rules, or the user?</li><li>How are wrong memories corrected or forgotten?</li><li>What must never be stored (personal or regulated data)?</li><li>How do you stop one bad memory contaminating future answers?</li></ul><div class=\"box key\"><div class=\"bx-h\">Say it in the room</div>'I treat the context window as a budget: keep it lean, summarise old turns, and retrieve long-term memory only when it's relevant, with clear rules on what is never stored.'</div>","checks":[{"q":"A 40-turn support chat is getting slow and expensive. The simplest effective fix:","opts":["Switch to a model with a larger context window","Restart the conversation every ten turns","Summarise older turns into a running summary","Send the full history to a cheaper model"],"a":2,"why":"Summarising older turns keeps the important facts while cutting tokens, cost and latency. A bigger window just pays for more tokens."},{"q":"Which type of agent memory stores stable facts about a specific user?","opts":["Working memory","Procedural memory","KV cache","Profile memory"],"a":3,"why":"Profile memory holds durable facts about the user or account, retrieved when relevant."}],"added":true}},{"mid":"m7","lesson":{"id":"l3","t":"Evaluation metrics in depth","min":8,"html":"<p class=\"lead\">Saying 'we'd run evals' isn't enough; you need to name the metric for each layer and know which ones mislead.</p><h3>Retrieval metrics</h3><p>recall@K, precision@K, MRR and nDCG measure whether the right chunks come back and how high they rank. Recall@K is the first one to check.</p><h3>RAG metrics (the RAGAS family)</h3><ul><li><strong>Context precision</strong>: was the retrieved context relevant?</li><li><strong>Context recall</strong>: did it contain everything needed to answer?</li><li><strong>Faithfulness</strong>: is every claim in the answer supported by the context?</li><li><strong>Answer relevance</strong>: does the answer actually address the question?</li></ul><h3>Generation metrics</h3><ul><li><strong>Exact match / F1</strong> for short factual answers</li><li><strong>BLEU / ROUGE</strong> (word overlap with a reference): weak for open-ended answers, because good answers can use different words</li><li><strong>LLM-as-judge</strong> with a rubric, calibrated against human labels</li><li><strong>Pointwise</strong> (score one answer) vs <strong>pairwise</strong> (pick the better of two, giving a win rate). Pairwise is usually more reliable for comparing versions</li></ul><h3>Classification metrics</h3><p>Precision, recall, F1 and the confusion matrix (true and false positives and negatives).</p><h3>Business metrics in production</h3><ul><li><strong>Containment or deflection rate</strong>: resolved without a human</li><li>Escalation rate, resolution rate, CSAT, thumbs-down rate</li><li><strong>Cost per resolved case</strong></li></ul><h3>Rigour</h3><ul><li>Use enough examples to trust a difference; a 2-point change on 30 questions may be noise</li><li>Outputs vary, so run several times and average</li><li>Keep a held-out test set you don't tune against</li></ul><div class=\"box key\"><div class=\"bx-h\">Say it in the room</div>'I'd measure retrieval with recall@K, generation with faithfulness and answer relevance, and the business with containment and cost per resolved case.'</div>","checks":[{"q":"An answer is fully supported by the retrieved chunks but doesn't address what the user asked. Which metric catches this?","opts":["Answer relevance","Faithfulness","Context recall","Recall@K"],"a":0,"why":"Faithfulness checks support from the context; answer relevance checks whether the answer addresses the question."},{"q":"Why is ROUGE a poor main metric for an open-ended assistant?","opts":["It is too expensive to compute at scale","It only measures word overlap with one reference","It cannot be automated in a CI pipeline","It requires a calibrated LLM judge to run"],"a":1,"why":"Word-overlap metrics penalise good answers phrased differently from the reference."}],"added":true}},{"mid":"m8","lesson":{"id":"l2","t":"Guardrails at input, output & action","min":7,"html":"<p class=\"lead\">Guardrails are the checks wrapped around a model. The useful way to present them is by where they sit, and the senior point is that every guardrail needs a graceful fallback.</p><h3>Three checkpoints</h3><ul><li><strong>Input</strong>: before the model sees the text</li><li><strong>Output</strong>: before the user sees the answer</li><li><strong>Action</strong>: before a tool changes anything</li></ul><h3>Input guardrails</h3><ul><li>PII detection and redaction</li><li>Moderation for abusive or harmful requests</li><li>Injection detection (useful, never sufficient)</li><li>Topic limits: 'only answer about our banking products'</li></ul><h3>Output guardrails</h3><ul><li>Schema validation of structured outputs</li><li><strong>Groundedness</strong>: every claim backed by a source, citations present</li><li>PII-leak and toxicity checks</li><li>Business rules: never quote prices, never give medical or legal advice</li></ul><h3>Action guardrails</h3><ul><li>Allow-lists of tools and destinations</li><li>Spend and volume limits</li><li>Human approval for irreversible actions</li></ul><h3>Trade-offs</h3><ul><li>Every check adds latency and cost, so <strong>tier them</strong>: cheap rules first, model-based checks only when needed</li><li>Over-strict guardrails make a useless product: a blunt 'never discuss prices' rule can kill a sales assistant's leads</li><li>Always pair a block with a way forward: offer a callback, escalate to a human, point to the right page</li></ul><div class=\"box key\"><div class=\"bx-h\">Say it in the room</div>'Guardrails sit at input, output and action. I'd tier them, cheap rules first, and make sure every refusal comes with a way forward.'</div>","checks":[{"q":"A guardrail stops a sales bot quoting prices, and leads drop sharply. The best fix keeps the rule and:","opts":["Removes the price documents from retrieval entirely","Adds a second guardrail that blocks pricing words","Offers a sales callback whenever price is asked","Switches to a larger and more cautious model"],"a":2,"why":"The rule is sound; the dead end is the problem. Turning price questions into callback offers keeps safety and recovers leads."},{"q":"Which is an output guardrail?","opts":["Redacting phone numbers from the user's message","Requiring approval before a refund is issued","Limiting which tools the agent can call","Checking every claim is backed by a cited source"],"a":3,"why":"Groundedness checks run on the answer before the user sees it. The others are input or action guardrails."}],"added":true}},{"mid":"m9","lesson":{"id":"l9","t":"Streaming, APIs & real-time protocols","min":7,"html":"<p class=\"lead\">How answers travel from the model to the user shapes perceived speed and system design. Expect 'SSE or WebSockets?' and 'how would you handle rate limits?'.</p><h3>Streaming</h3><p>Send tokens as they're generated. It doesn't make generation faster; it makes the first words appear in about a second instead of after the whole answer. It's the biggest perceived-latency fix for any chat product.</p><h3>SSE vs WebSockets</h3><div class=\"tblwrap\"><table class=\"tbl\"><tr><th></th><th>SSE (server-sent events)</th><th>WebSockets</th></tr><tr><td>Direction</td><td>One-way: server to client</td><td>Two-way: both sides, any time</td></tr><tr><td>Transport</td><td>Plain HTTP, auto-reconnect</td><td>Persistent upgraded connection</td></tr><tr><td>Typical use</td><td>Streaming an LLM answer</td><td>Real-time voice, live collaboration</td></tr></table></div><p>The major LLM APIs stream over SSE. A real-time voice agent, where audio flows both ways continuously, uses WebSockets (or WebRTC).</p><h3>Long-running jobs</h3><ul><li><strong>Polling</strong>: the client asks 'done yet?' periodically</li><li><strong>Webhooks</strong>: the server calls your endpoint when the job finishes</li><li><strong>Batch APIs</strong>: submit many requests asynchronously at roughly half price</li></ul><h3>Rate limits</h3><p>Providers limit requests per minute (<strong>RPM</strong>) and tokens per minute (<strong>TPM</strong>). A <strong>429</strong> error means slow down: back off exponentially with jitter, queue requests, and request higher limits for production.</p><h3>Function calling, step by step</h3><ol><li>Send the tools (name, description, parameter schema) with the request</li><li>The model replies with a structured tool call</li><li>Your code runs the tool</li><li>You send the result back</li><li>The model answers or calls another tool</li></ol><div class=\"box key\"><div class=\"bx-h\">Say it in the room</div>'One-way is SSE, two-way is WebSockets: a chatbot streaming its answer uses SSE, a real-time voice agent uses WebSockets or WebRTC.'</div>","checks":[{"q":"A chatbot needs to stream its answer to the browser. The standard choice is:","opts":["SSE, since the data only flows from server to client","WebSockets, since streaming needs a persistent two-way connection","Polling every 500 ms for newly generated tokens","A webhook that fires when each token is ready"],"a":0,"why":"Streaming an answer is one-way from server to client, which is exactly what SSE does over plain HTTP."},{"q":"Your app gets frequent HTTP 429 errors from the model provider. First response:","opts":["Retry immediately in a tight loop until it succeeds","Back off exponentially with jitter and queue requests","Switch every request to the batch API instead","Increase the max_tokens setting on each call"],"a":1,"why":"429 means rate-limited. Exponential backoff with jitter and a queue smooth the load; immediate retries make it worse."}],"added":true}},{"mid":"m11","lesson":{"id":"l2","t":"Fairness, bias & explainability","min":7,"html":"<p class=\"lead\">Any system that touches people's opportunities (hiring, credit, insurance, pricing) will be asked 'is it fair, and can you explain it?'. You need the vocabulary and an honest answer.</p><h3>Where bias comes from</h3><ul><li><strong>Historical bias</strong>: the data reflects past unfair decisions</li><li><strong>Representation bias</strong>: some groups are under-represented in the data</li><li><strong>Measurement bias</strong>: the label is a poor proxy (arrests instead of crimes)</li><li><strong>Proxy variables</strong>: postcode or school standing in for protected traits even when those are removed</li></ul><h3>Fairness definitions</h3><ul><li><strong>Demographic parity</strong>: groups receive positive outcomes at similar rates</li><li><strong>Equal opportunity</strong>: qualified people in each group are approved at similar rates (equal true-positive rates)</li><li><strong>Equalised odds</strong>: similar true-positive and false-positive rates across groups</li></ul><div class=\"box warnbx\"><div class=\"bx-h\">Watch out</div>These definitions can conflict mathematically; you can't satisfy all of them at once. Choosing one is a policy decision for the client, not a technical default.</div><h3>Testing and mitigation</h3><ul><li>Measure outcomes and error rates <strong>per group</strong>, not just overall</li><li>Mitigate before training (rebalance data), during (fairness constraints) or after (adjust thresholds per policy)</li><li>Keep a human review path and an appeal route for affected people</li></ul><h3>Explainability</h3><ul><li><strong>Global</strong>: what drives the model overall (feature importance)</li><li><strong>Local</strong>: why this person got this decision (SHAP values, counterfactuals: 'approved if income were X higher')</li><li>Simpler models are easier to explain; regulators may require explanations for individual decisions</li></ul><h3>LLM-specific bias</h3><p>LLMs can stereotype, treat names or dialects differently, and judge text unevenly. Test with paired prompts that differ only in a name or dialect.</p><div class=\"box key\"><div class=\"bx-h\">Say it in the room</div>'I'd measure outcomes and error rates per group, agree with the client which fairness definition applies because they conflict, and make sure individual decisions can be explained and appealed.'</div>","checks":[{"q":"A credit model never uses nationality, but approval rates still differ sharply by nationality. Most likely cause:","opts":["Demographic parity was applied too strictly","The model has too few features to be accurate","Proxy variables such as postcode carry the same signal","Equalised odds were enforced during training"],"a":2,"why":"Removing a protected attribute doesn't remove its signal. Correlated proxies like postcode can reproduce the same disparity."},{"q":"Which explains why one specific applicant was rejected?","opts":["Global feature importance across the dataset","The model's overall accuracy on the test set","Demographic parity across all applicant groups","A local explanation such as SHAP values or a counterfactual"],"a":3,"why":"Local explanations describe an individual decision; global importance describes the model overall."}],"added":true}}],"module":{"id":"m14","n":"14","spine":false,"t":"Multimodal, Voice & Arabic AI","sub":"Beyond English text: Arabic and dialects, documents and images, and real-time voice agents, the topics that decide whether a GCC deployment actually works.","lessons":[{"id":"l1","t":"Arabic & multilingual AI","min":8,"html":"<p class=\"lead\">In the Gulf, 'does it work in Arabic?' is often the deciding question, and most global vendors answer it badly. Knowing the specific failure points is a genuine differentiator.</p><h3>The language landscape</h3><ul><li><strong>MSA</strong> (Modern Standard Arabic): formal, written, news and documents</li><li><strong>Dialects</strong>: Gulf, Levantine, Egyptian, Maghrebi and more. How people actually speak and type</li><li><strong>Code-switching</strong>: Arabic and English mixed in one sentence</li><li><strong>Arabizi</strong>: Arabic in Latin letters and numbers ('7abibi', '3arabi')</li></ul><h3>Technical issues</h3><ul><li><strong>Token tax</strong>: Arabic typically needs 1.5 to 2.5× the tokens of English for the same meaning, raising cost and latency and shrinking effective context</li><li><strong>Normalisation</strong>: alef variants (أ إ آ ا), ta marbuta, optional diacritics and elongation all need standardising before keyword search</li><li><strong>Right-to-left</strong> display and mixed-direction text in interfaces</li><li><strong>Speech recognition</strong> is noticeably weaker on dialects than on MSA</li></ul><h3>Retrieval</h3><ul><li>Use an explicitly <strong>multilingual embedding model</strong></li><li>Test <strong>cross-lingual</strong> retrieval: an English question should find an Arabic document and vice versa</li><li>Normalise text before BM25 so spelling variants match</li></ul><h3>Evaluation</h3><ul><li>Build golden sets <strong>per dialect</strong>, labelled by native speakers</li><li>LLM judges can favour MSA or English phrasing, so calibrate them on Arabic</li><li>Report quality by language and dialect, not just one overall score</li></ul><h3>Regional context</h3><ul><li>Arabic-centric models exist, such as Jais and ALLaM; test them alongside global models on your own data</li><li>Data residency rules (UAE and Saudi PDPL) often require in-region processing</li></ul><div class=\"box key\"><div class=\"bx-h\">Say it in the room</div>'For Arabic I'd budget a token multiplier, use a multilingual embedding model with text normalisation, and evaluate per dialect with native-speaker labels.'</div>","checks":[{"q":"An Arabic support bot scores well overall but Gulf-dialect users complain. What evaluation change exposes this?","opts":["Report results per dialect with native-speaker labels","Increase the overall golden set size","Translate all test questions into MSA first","Use BLEU instead of an LLM judge"],"a":0,"why":"An overall score hides weak dialects. Per-dialect golden sets labelled by native speakers show where it fails."},{"q":"Keyword search misses Arabic documents that clearly contain the search term. Likely cause:","opts":["The vector index is too small for Arabic text","Spelling variants such as alef forms weren't normalised","The embedding model has too many dimensions","Arabic documents need larger chunk sizes"],"a":1,"why":"BM25 matches exact strings, so unnormalised variants of the same word don't match. Normalise before indexing."}],"added":true},{"id":"l2","t":"Documents & vision: OCR to multimodal models","min":7,"html":"<p class=\"lead\">A huge share of enterprise data is PDFs, scans, invoices, forms and screenshots. Getting it into an AI system reliably is often the real project.</p><h3>The options</h3><ul><li><strong>Classic OCR</strong>: turns images of text into text. Cheap and fast, but loses layout and struggles with tables and handwriting</li><li><strong>Layout-aware document AI</strong>: extracts text with its structure (headings, tables, key-value fields). Services like Azure Document Intelligence, Google Document AI, AWS Textract</li><li><strong>Vision-language models (VLMs)</strong>: multimodal LLMs that read images directly and answer questions or extract fields. Flexible, handle messy layouts, cost more per page</li></ul><h3>Choosing</h3><ul><li>High-volume, fixed forms (invoices from known suppliers) → document AI with templates</li><li>Varied, messy documents → VLM extraction into a schema</li><li>Search over documents → parse to text with structure, then chunk and embed</li><li>Often combine: OCR or document AI first, VLM for the hard pages</li></ul><h3>Extraction done right</h3><ul><li>Extract into a <strong>strict schema</strong> and validate every field</li><li>Return <strong>confidence</strong> per field; route low-confidence pages to a human</li><li>Keep a link from every extracted value back to the page and region it came from</li><li>Measure <strong>field-level accuracy</strong> on a labelled set of real documents</li></ul><h3>Arabic documents</h3><p>Arabic OCR quality varies widely, especially for scans, handwriting and mixed Arabic-English forms. Test on the client's real documents before committing.</p><div class=\"box key\"><div class=\"bx-h\">Say it in the room</div>'For fixed high-volume forms I'd use document AI; for messy, varied documents a vision-language model extracting into a validated schema, with low-confidence fields sent to a human.'</div>","checks":[{"q":"A client receives thousands of invoices a day from 15 known suppliers with fixed layouts. Best fit:","opts":["A vision-language model reading every page","Manual data entry with spot checks","Layout-aware document AI with templates","Plain OCR followed by keyword search"],"a":2,"why":"Fixed, high-volume layouts suit cheaper template-based document AI; VLMs earn their cost on varied, messy documents."},{"q":"How should low-confidence extracted fields be handled?","opts":["Drop them from the output silently","Re-run the same model until confident","Fill them with the most common value","Route them to a human reviewer"],"a":3,"why":"Per-field confidence lets you send only uncertain values to a human, keeping accuracy high without reviewing everything."}],"added":true},{"id":"l3","t":"Voice AI: the real-time pipeline","min":8,"html":"<p class=\"lead\">Voice agents are among the most demanded GCC use cases (call centres, booking lines) and among the hardest to make feel natural. The whole design is a latency budget.</p><h3>The cascaded pipeline</h3><ol><li><strong>VAD</strong> (voice activity detection): detects when the user starts and stops speaking</li><li><strong>ASR / speech-to-text</strong>: turns audio into text</li><li><strong>LLM</strong>: decides what to say (often with tools and RAG)</li><li><strong>TTS / text-to-speech</strong>: turns the reply into audio</li></ol><h3>Speech-to-speech models</h3><p>Newer models take audio in and produce audio out directly, cutting latency and keeping tone of voice, but they're harder to control, inspect and evaluate than the cascaded pipeline.</p><h3>The latency budget</h3><p>People notice gaps over roughly a second. Every stage must stream:</p><ul><li>Streaming ASR that emits partial transcripts</li><li>The LLM's first tokens go straight to TTS</li><li>TTS starts speaking before the full sentence is written</li><li>Short, spoken-style answers (no bullet lists read aloud)</li></ul><h3>What makes it feel human</h3><ul><li><strong>Barge-in</strong>: the user can interrupt and the agent stops talking</li><li><strong>Turn detection</strong>: not cutting people off mid-pause</li><li>Handling noise, accents and <strong>dialects</strong></li><li>Confirming critical details back ('That's the 14th, correct?')</li></ul><h3>Transport</h3><p>Real-time audio flows both ways continuously, so it uses <strong>WebSockets</strong> or <strong>WebRTC</strong>, not SSE.</p><h3>Measuring it</h3><ul><li>Word error rate (WER) per dialect for ASR</li><li>End-to-end response latency (p50 and p95)</li><li>Task completion, containment and transfer-to-human rate</li></ul><div class=\"box key\"><div class=\"bx-h\">Say it in the room</div>'A voice agent is a latency budget: VAD, streaming ASR, LLM and streaming TTS, over WebSockets or WebRTC, with barge-in and per-dialect word error rate as the metrics I'd watch.'</div>","checks":[{"q":"Callers complain the voice agent keeps talking when they try to interrupt. Which capability is missing?","opts":["Barge-in handling","Speech-to-speech generation","Prompt caching","Text normalisation"],"a":0,"why":"Barge-in lets the user interrupt: the agent detects speech and stops its own playback."},{"q":"Which protocol fits a real-time voice agent?","opts":["SSE, since the server streams audio to the client","WebSockets or WebRTC, since audio flows both ways","Polling every second for new audio chunks","A batch API that processes calls overnight"],"a":1,"why":"Voice needs continuous two-way audio, which SSE can't do."}],"added":true}],"exam":[{"q":"Arabic text typically costs more to process than English because:","opts":["Providers charge a premium for non-English languages","Arabic models run on more expensive hardware","It usually needs more tokens for the same meaning","Right-to-left text must be processed twice"],"a":2,"why":"Tokenizers trained mostly on English split Arabic into more tokens, raising cost and latency."},{"q":"What does cross-lingual retrieval allow?","opts":["Translating every document before indexing","Searching two indexes and merging the results","Keyword search that ignores diacritics","An English question finding an Arabic document"],"a":3,"why":"Multilingual embeddings place equivalent meanings near each other across languages."},{"q":"For varied, messy scanned documents, the most flexible extraction approach is:","opts":["A vision-language model into a validated schema","Classic OCR with keyword rules","Template-based document AI for each layout","Manual review of every single page"],"a":0,"why":"VLMs handle varied layouts; a strict schema and validation keep the output reliable."},{"q":"In a cascaded voice pipeline, what comes immediately after voice activity detection?","opts":["Text-to-speech","Speech recognition (ASR)","The LLM response","Barge-in detection"],"a":1,"why":"VAD detects speech; ASR then turns it into text for the LLM."},{"q":"What is the main advantage of speech-to-speech models over a cascaded pipeline?","opts":["They are easier to evaluate and inspect","They never need any guardrails at all","Lower latency and preserved tone of voice","They work without any network connection"],"a":2,"why":"Going audio-to-audio removes stages, cutting latency and keeping prosody, at the cost of control and inspectability."},{"q":"The best ASR metric to report for a Gulf call centre is:","opts":["Overall BLEU score","Average tokens per call","Cache hit rate","Word error rate per dialect"],"a":3,"why":"Word error rate broken down by dialect shows where recognition actually fails."}]},"notes":{"m1":["## Caching: KV cache\n- **Inside one request**, automatic\n- Stores Keys/Values so the model doesn't recompute past tokens\n- Not a fix you propose, it's how inference works\n\n## Caching: Prompt (prefix) caching\n- **Provider feature, across requests**\n- Reuses the processing of an **identical prompt start**\n- Cached reads ≈ 90% cheaper + faster first token\n- Model still writes a **fresh answer** every time\n- Rules: fixed content first (system, tools, docs), variable last (question)\n- Expires after a short time if unused\n\n## Caching: Response cache (exact match)\n- Your system stores full answers keyed on the exact question (hash)\n- Skips the model entirely, only hits on identical input\n\n## Caching: Semantic cache\n- Embed the question → if a past question is **similar enough**, return its stored answer\n- Biggest savings, biggest risk\n! \"Refund for a laptop?\" ≈ \"refund for a phone?\" but the answers differ\n- Only for **stable** answers (FAQs, policies), with a strict threshold\n- Never for personal or live data (\"where's my order\")\n\n## Caching: Invalidation\n- Time limit (TTL) + clear on document change (version keys)\n\n> \"KV cache is inside one call, prompt caching reuses an identical prefix across calls, and response or semantic caching skips the model by reusing a stored answer.\"\n✎ A 4-row table: CACHE · WHERE · SAVES · RISK","## Prompting: core techniques\n- **Zero-shot** first · **few-shot** (2–5 well-chosen examples)\n- Say who it's for and what it's used for\n- Separate instructions / context / question\n- Specify the output schema (or structured outputs)\n## Prompting: reasoning\n- **CoT** on non-reasoning models only\n- **Self-consistency**: sample several, take majority\n- **Chaining**: extract → classify → draft\n- **ReAct** (agents) · **self-critique**\n## Prompting: hygiene\n- Version in git, test vs golden set, one change at a time\n- Critical rules at start + end\n! Drop: threats, bribes, persona padding, CoT on reasoning models\n> \"Zero-shot with clear structure first, add examples if needed, chain complex tasks, keep only what the evals prove.\""],"m2":["## MLOps: the lifecycle\n1. Features on a schedule\n2. Train → validate → **register** (data version + metrics)\n3. Deploy: **batch** (nightly scores) or **real-time** (API)\n4. Monitor → retrain\n## MLOps: building blocks\n- **Feature store**: same features for training and live → no **training-serving skew**\n- **Model registry** · **experiment tracking** (MLflow)\n## MLOps: what breaks\n- **Data drift**: inputs change\n- **Concept drift**: the relationship changes\n- **Label delay**: monitor inputs + predictions meanwhile\n- **Feedback loops**: the model changes its own future data\n> \"Register with the data version, monitor drift because labels arrive late, retrain on schedule or on a drift threshold.\"\n✎ Loop: DATA → TRAIN → REGISTER → DEPLOY → MONITOR → (drift!) → back to TRAIN"],"m3":["## Text-to-SQL: The flow\n1. User question\n2. **Retrieve relevant schema** (tables, columns, descriptions), not the whole warehouse\n3. LLM writes SQL (with a few example question → SQL pairs)\n4. **Validate**: parses, read-only, allowed tables, LIMIT, cost check\n5. Run it\n6. Answer in words + **show the SQL** and the numbers\n\n## Text-to-SQL: Why it goes wrong\n- Ambiguous metrics (\"revenue\": gross? net? which date?)\n- Wrong joins → duplicated rows\n- Missing filters (test accounts, currency)\n- Huge, expensive queries\n\n## Text-to-SQL: Fixes\n- **Semantic layer**: one agreed definition per metric\n- Column descriptions + example queries\n- Ask a **clarifying question** when ambiguous\n- **Read-only** service account, row-level security\n- Dry run to estimate cost (bytes scanned) before running\n\n## Text-to-SQL: Evaluate\n- **Execution accuracy**: does the result match the expected result? (not string match on SQL)\n- Golden set of real business questions\n\n> \"The hard part of text-to-SQL isn't writing SQL, it's agreeing what 'revenue' means. I'd put a semantic layer under it and evaluate on execution accuracy.\"\n✎ Pipeline: QUESTION → SCHEMA → SQL → ✓ VALIDATE → RUN → ANSWER + SQL"],"m4":["## Chunking: Why chunking matters\n- A chunk = the **unit you retrieve**. Retrieval can't return half a chunk\n- Too big → several topics blur into one vector, more noise and cost\n- Too small → loses context (\"30 days\" but for which product?)\n- Most \"bad RAG\" is **bad chunking**\n\n## Chunking: The strategies (simple → advanced)\n1. **Fixed-size**: every N tokens + overlap. Simple, but cuts mid-sentence\n2. **Recursive** (default): split on paragraphs → sentences → words until under the size limit\n3. **Sentence / paragraph**: natural boundaries, uneven sizes\n4. **Structure-aware**: split on headings, sections, slides, functions. Best for manuals, policies, code\n5. **Semantic**: embed sentences, cut where meaning shifts (similarity drops). Costlier, gains vary, test it\n6. **Parent-child (small-to-big)**: retrieve small precise chunks, send the **whole parent section** to the LLM\n7. **Sentence window**: retrieve one sentence, add its neighbours\n8. **Contextual chunking**: prepend a short LLM-written line saying where the chunk sits (\"From the 2026 refund policy, electronics section…\")\n9. **Proposition / agentic**: an LLM rewrites text into atomic facts. Precise, expensive\n10. **Late chunking**: embed the whole document first, then split the embeddings, so each chunk keeps document context\n\n## Chunking: The knobs\n- **Size**: ~200–1,000 tokens (default 400–600)\n- **Overlap**: 10–20% so ideas aren't cut in half\n- Must fit the **embedding model's max input**\n- **Metadata** on every chunk: source, title, **heading path**, date, language, permissions\n\n## Chunking: Special content\n- **Tables**: keep whole, or one row per chunk with the column headers repeated\n- **PDFs**: parsing quality first (layout, OCR, headers/footers)\n- **FAQs**: one question + answer per chunk\n- **Code**: split by function or class\n- **Arabic**: sentence splitter that knows Arabic punctuation\n\n## Chunking: Symptoms → fix\n- Answer split across two chunks → more overlap or parent-child\n- Chunk lacks context → heading breadcrumbs or contextual chunking\n- Tables garbled → structure-aware parsing\n- How to choose: test 2–3 configs on the golden set, compare **recall@K**\n\n> \"I'd start with recursive splitting around 500 tokens with overlap and heading metadata, then test structure-aware or parent-child chunking against recall@K on our golden set.\"\n✎ A document split three ways: fixed (cuts mid-sentence ✗), recursive (clean paragraphs ✓), parent-child (small boxes inside a big box)","## Retrieval maths: Embedding dimensions\n- Common sizes: 384 · 768 · 1,024 · 1,536 · 3,072\n- More dims → more nuance, but **more storage + slower search**\n- **Matryoshka** embeddings: can be shortened with little loss\n\n## Retrieval maths: Storage maths\n- Size = vectors × dims × **4 bytes** (float32)\n- 1M × 1,536 × 4 ≈ **6 GB** (+ index overhead)\n- **Quantisation**: float16 = ½ · int8 = ¼ · binary = 1/32 (some recall loss)\n\n## Retrieval maths: Index types\n- **Flat**: exact, checks everything, fine for small sets\n- **IVF**: clusters vectors, searches nearest clusters\n- **HNSW**: layered graph, fast, high recall, memory-hungry (default)\n- **PQ**: compresses vectors to save memory\n\n## Retrieval maths: Getting the right top-K\n- **Metadata filters** (date, product, permissions): pre-filter vs post-filter\n- **Top-K**: retrieve ~20–50, rerank to ~5\n- **MMR**: diversify results so you don't get 5 near-duplicates\n- **Hybrid** (vector + BM25, fused with RRF)\n\n## Retrieval maths: Encoders\n- **Bi-encoder**: query and doc embedded separately (fast retrieval)\n- **Cross-encoder**: read together (accurate reranking)\n- **ColBERT**: compares word-level vectors (middle ground)\n\n## Retrieval maths: Retrieval metrics\n- **recall@K**: was the right chunk in the top K?\n- **precision@K**: how much of the top K was relevant?\n- **MRR**: how high was the first right answer?\n- **nDCG**: rewards the best items ranked highest\n\n> \"One million 1,536-dimension vectors is about 6 GB in float32, so at scale I'd look at quantisation or smaller dimensions.\"\n✎ The storage sum written out: 1,000,000 × 1,536 × 4 B ≈ 6 GB"],"m5":["## Training: The pipeline\n1. **Pre-training**: predict the next token on huge text → knowledge, no manners\n2. **SFT** (supervised fine-tuning): example prompts + ideal answers → follows instructions\n3. **Preference tuning**: learn which answers people prefer\n- **RLHF**: train a reward model, then optimise against it\n- **DPO**: learn directly from better-vs-worse answer pairs (simpler)\n\n## Training: Cheaper tuning\n- **LoRA**: train small add-on matrices instead of all weights\n- **QLoRA**: LoRA on a quantised (compressed) base model → fits on smaller GPUs\n- **Distillation**: big model teaches a small one a narrow task\n\n## Training: Quantisation\n- Store weights in fewer bits: 16 → 8 → 4\n- Less memory, faster, small quality loss\n- How big models run on one GPU or a laptop\n\n## Training: Risks\n- **Catastrophic forgetting**: tuning on narrow data degrades general skills\n- Overfitting to a small dataset\n- Retraining every time the base model changes\n\n> \"Pre-training gives knowledge, SFT teaches it to follow instructions, and preference tuning (RLHF or DPO) teaches it which answers people prefer.\"\n✎ Three steps: PRE-TRAIN (book icon) → SFT (Q&A icon) → PREFERENCE (👍👎)"],"m6":["## Context & memory: Context window\n- Everything the model sees in one call: system prompt + tools + history + retrieved docs + **its own output**\n- Bigger windows still suffer **lost-in-the-middle** and cost more\n- Treat it as a **budget**\n\n## Context & memory: Managing long conversations\n- **Sliding window**: keep the last N turns\n- **Summarise** older turns into a running summary\n- **Compaction**: rewrite the history shorter when it gets long\n- Keep stable content first for **prompt caching**\n\n## Context & memory: Memory types\n- **Short-term**: this conversation / task\n- **Long-term**: facts and past episodes in a store, retrieved when relevant\n- **Profile**: stable facts about the user\n- **Procedural**: how to do things (learned instructions)\n\n## Context & memory: Memory design questions\n- What gets saved, and who decides?\n- When is it forgotten or corrected?\n- Privacy: what must never be stored?\n- How do you stop wrong memories spreading?\n\n> \"I treat the context window as a budget: keep it lean, summarise old turns, and retrieve long-term memory only when relevant.\"\n✎ A bar split into colored blocks: SYSTEM · TOOLS · HISTORY · DOCS · OUTPUT, with a line marking the limit"],"m7":["## Eval metrics: Retrieval\n- recall@K · precision@K · MRR · nDCG · hit rate\n\n## Eval metrics: RAG (RAGAS-style)\n- **Context precision**: was the retrieved context relevant?\n- **Context recall**: did it contain everything needed?\n- **Faithfulness**: is every claim backed by the context?\n- **Answer relevance**: does it actually answer the question?\n\n## Eval metrics: Generation\n- **Exact match / F1** for short factual answers\n- **BLEU / ROUGE** (word overlap): weak for open-ended answers\n- **LLM-as-judge**: rubric, calibrated vs humans\n- **Pointwise** (score one answer) vs **pairwise** (A vs B → win rate)\n\n## Eval metrics: Classification\n- Precision · recall · F1 · confusion matrix\n\n## Eval metrics: Business (online)\n- Containment / deflection rate · escalation rate\n- Resolution rate · CSAT · thumbs-down rate\n- Cost per resolved case\n\n## Eval metrics: Rigour\n- Enough examples to trust a difference\n- Run several times (outputs vary), report the average\n- Separate dev set from test set\n\n> \"I'd measure retrieval with recall@K, generation with faithfulness and answer relevance, and the business with containment and cost per resolved case.\"\n✎ Three columns: RETRIEVAL | GENERATION | BUSINESS, with 3 metrics under each"],"m8":["## Guardrails: Three checkpoints\n- **Input**: before the model sees it\n- **Output**: before the user sees it\n- **Action**: before a tool does something\n\n## Guardrails: Input guardrails\n- **PII detection / redaction**\n- Moderation (abuse, harmful requests)\n- Injection detection\n- Topic limits (\"only answer about banking\")\n\n## Guardrails: Output guardrails\n- **Schema validation**\n- **Groundedness**: claims backed by sources, citations present\n- PII leak and toxicity checks\n- Business rules (never quote prices, never give medical advice)\n\n## Guardrails: Action guardrails\n- Allow-lists of tools and destinations\n- Spending / volume limits\n- **Human approval** for irreversible actions\n\n## Guardrails: Trade-offs\n- Every check adds latency and cost → **tier them** (cheap rules first, model checks only when needed)\n- Too strict → useless bot (the real estate case: a blunt rule killed 40% of leads)\n- Always offer a way forward: escalate to a human, offer a callback\n\n> \"Guardrails sit at three points: input, output and action, and each one needs a graceful fallback, not just a refusal.\"\n✎ A pipe with three gates: INPUT 🚧 → MODEL → OUTPUT 🚧 → TOOL 🚧"],"m9":["## Streaming & APIs: Streaming\n- Send tokens **as they're generated**\n- Doesn't make generation faster, makes it **feel** faster (first word in ~1 s)\n- The biggest perceived-latency fix\n\n## Streaming & APIs: SSE vs WebSockets\n- **SSE** (server-sent events): **one-way**, server → browser, plain HTTP, auto-reconnect\n- **WebSockets**: **two-way**, persistent, both sides talk anytime\n- Streaming an LLM answer → **SSE** (what the major LLM APIs use)\n- Real-time voice, live collaboration → **WebSockets**\n\n## Streaming & APIs: Long jobs\n- **Polling**: client asks \"done yet?\"\n- **Webhooks**: server calls you when done\n- **Batch API**: async, ~50% cheaper\n\n## Streaming & APIs: Rate limits\n- Requests per minute (**RPM**) + tokens per minute (**TPM**)\n- **429** error → back off with jitter and retry\n\n## Streaming & APIs: Function calling flow\n1. Send tools (name, description, schema)\n2. Model replies with a tool call (JSON)\n3. Your code runs it\n4. Send the result back\n5. Model answers or calls another tool\n\n> \"One-way is SSE, two-way is WebSockets. A chatbot streaming its answer uses SSE; a real-time voice app uses WebSockets.\"\n✎ Two arrows: SSE = one arrow server → client; WebSocket = arrows both ways"],"m11":["## Fairness: where bias comes from\n- Historical · representation · measurement · **proxy variables** (postcode ≈ protected trait)\n## Fairness: definitions\n- **Demographic parity**: similar approval rates\n- **Equal opportunity**: similar approval for qualified people\n- **Equalised odds**: similar true + false positive rates\n! They conflict: picking one is a **policy** decision\n## Fairness: what to do\n- Measure outcomes + errors **per group**\n- Mitigate before / during / after training\n- Human review + appeal route\n## Explainability\n- **Global**: what drives the model\n- **Local**: why this person (SHAP, counterfactuals)\n- LLMs: test paired prompts differing only by name or dialect\n> \"Measure per group, agree which fairness definition applies, and make every decision explainable and appealable.\""],"m14":["## Arabic: The language landscape\n- **MSA** (formal) vs dialects: Gulf, Levantine, Egyptian, Maghrebi\n- **Code-switching**: Arabic + English in one sentence\n- **Arabizi**: Arabic in Latin letters and numbers (\"7abibi\")\n\n## Arabic: Technical issues\n- **Token tax**: ~1.5–2.5× English → more cost, latency, less context\n- Normalisation: alef forms (أ إ آ ا), ta marbuta, diacritics\n- Right-to-left display\n- Speech recognition is weaker on dialects\n\n## Arabic: Retrieval\n- Use a **multilingual embedding model**\n- Test **cross-lingual** search (English question → Arabic doc)\n- Normalise text before keyword search\n\n## Arabic: Evaluation\n- Golden set **per dialect**, labelled by native speakers\n- LLM judges may favour MSA, calibrate them\n- Track quality by language, not just overall\n\n## Arabic: Regional context\n- Arabic-centric models exist (e.g. Jais, ALLaM)\n- Data residency: UAE and Saudi PDPL\n\n> \"For Arabic I'd budget a token multiplier, pick a multilingual embedding model, and evaluate per dialect with native-speaker labels.\"\n✎ A map of the region with dialect labels: GULF · LEVANTINE · EGYPTIAN · MAGHREBI","## Documents: the options\n- **OCR**: cheap, loses layout\n- **Document AI** (Textract, Document Intelligence, Document AI): structure, tables, fields\n- **Vision-language models**: read images directly, flexible, pricier\n## Documents: choosing\n- Fixed high-volume forms → document AI\n- Messy varied docs → VLM into a schema\n- Search → parse with structure, then chunk\n## Documents: extraction done right\n- **Strict schema** + validation\n- **Confidence per field** → low confidence to a human\n- Link each value to its page and region\n- Measure **field-level accuracy**\n> \"Document AI for fixed forms, a VLM into a validated schema for messy ones, humans on low-confidence fields.\"","## Voice: the pipeline\n1. **VAD** (is someone speaking?)\n2. **ASR** (speech → text)\n3. **LLM**\n4. **TTS** (text → speech)\n- Or **speech-to-speech** models: faster, keeps tone, harder to control\n## Voice: latency budget (~1 s)\n- Stream every stage: partial ASR → first LLM tokens → TTS starts early\n- Short spoken answers\n## Voice: feels human\n- **Barge-in** (user can interrupt)\n- Turn detection · noise · **dialects**\n- Read back critical details\n## Voice: transport + metrics\n- **WebSockets / WebRTC** (two-way)\n- **WER per dialect** · p95 latency · completion · transfer rate\n> \"A voice agent is a latency budget: VAD, streaming ASR, LLM, streaming TTS over WebSockets, with barge-in.\"\n✎ Four boxes in a row: VAD → ASR → LLM → TTS, with \"~1 s\" written over the whole row"]},"cards":{"m1":[["KV cache vs prompt cache","KV cache works inside one request automatically. Prompt caching is a provider feature that reuses an identical prompt prefix across requests."],["Response cache","Your system stores the full answer for an exact question and returns it without calling the model."],["Semantic cache","Returns a stored answer when a new question is similar enough to an old one. Big savings, risky for near-miss questions; use only for stable FAQs."],["Cache invalidation","Clearing cached answers when they may be stale: by time limit (TTL) or when the source documents change."],["Why a semantic cache is risky","'Refund for a laptop?' and 'refund for a phone?' look similar but have different answers, so a loose threshold returns wrong answers confidently."],["Zero-shot prompting","Clear instructions with no examples. The place to start."],["Few-shot prompting","Including 2 to 5 worked examples in the prompt. Choosing examples that cover tricky cases matters more than adding more."],["Self-consistency","Sampling several answers and taking the majority, for higher accuracy at higher cost."],["Prompt chaining","Splitting a big task into a sequence of small, testable LLM calls, e.g. extract → classify → draft."],["Chain-of-thought (CoT)","Asking a model to reason step by step before answering. Helps non-reasoning models; redundant on reasoning models."]],"m2":[["Feature store","One place where features are defined once and served identically for training and live scoring."],["Training-serving skew","When features are computed differently in production than in training, so a good model performs badly live."],["Model registry","A catalogue of model versions with their training data, parameters, metrics and approval status."],["Data drift","The model's inputs change over time, e.g. a new customer mix."],["Concept drift","The relationship between inputs and the outcome changes, so learned patterns stop holding."],["Label delay","True outcomes arrive weeks after predictions, so you monitor inputs and prediction distributions in the meantime."],["Batch vs real-time scoring","Batch writes scores to a table on a schedule (churn); real-time scores each request via an API (fraud)."]],"m3":[["Text-to-SQL","An LLM turns a plain-English question into a SQL query, runs it and explains the result."],["Schema retrieval","Giving the model only the relevant tables and column descriptions, not the whole warehouse."],["SQL validation","Checking generated SQL before running it: it parses, is read-only, touches allowed tables, has a LIMIT, and won't cost too much."],["Execution accuracy","Evaluating text-to-SQL by whether the query returns the right result, not whether the SQL text matches."],["Why text-to-SQL fails","Ambiguous metric definitions, wrong joins that duplicate rows, missing filters, and expensive queries."],["Semantic layer (for text-to-SQL)","One agreed definition per business metric, so the model doesn't guess what 'revenue' or 'active user' means."]],"m4":[["Fixed-size chunking","Splitting every N tokens with some overlap. Simple, but often cuts sentences in half."],["Recursive chunking","Splitting on paragraphs, then sentences, then words until each piece is under the size limit. The production default."],["Structure-aware chunking","Splitting on the document's own structure: headings, sections, slides or functions. Best for manuals, policies and code."],["Semantic chunking","Embedding sentences and cutting where the meaning shifts. Costlier, and gains vary, so test it."],["Parent-child (small-to-big)","Retrieve small, precise chunks but send the whole parent section to the model, so it gets full context."],["Sentence-window retrieval","Retrieve one matching sentence, then include the sentences around it."],["Contextual chunking","Prepending a short LLM-written note to each chunk saying where it sits in the document, so it keeps its context."],["Proposition chunking","An LLM rewrites text into small standalone facts before embedding. Precise but expensive."],["Late chunking","Embedding the whole document first, then splitting the result into chunks, so each chunk keeps document-wide context."],["Chunk overlap","Repeating 10 to 20% of text between neighbouring chunks so ideas aren't cut in half."],["Heading breadcrumbs","Adding the section path (e.g. Policy › Refunds › Electronics) to a chunk so a fragment like '14 days' keeps its meaning."],["Chunking tables","Keep the table whole, or make one chunk per row with the column headers repeated."],["Embedding dimensions trade-off","More dimensions capture more nuance but cost more storage and slow search. Common sizes run from 384 to 3,072."],["Vector storage formula","Vectors × dimensions × 4 bytes (float32). 1M vectors × 1,536 dims ≈ 6 GB before index overhead."],["Vector quantisation","Storing vectors in fewer bits (float16, int8, binary) to cut memory, at some cost in recall."],["Matryoshka embeddings","Embeddings trained so they can be truncated to fewer dimensions with little quality loss."],["Flat index","Exact search that compares the query with every vector. Fine for small collections."],["IVF index","Groups vectors into clusters and only searches the clusters nearest the query."],["Pre-filter vs post-filter","Applying metadata filters before the vector search (accurate) or after it (may leave too few results)."],["MMR","Maximal marginal relevance: picks results that are relevant but different from each other, avoiding near-duplicates."],["ColBERT","A retrieval model that compares word-level vectors between query and document: more accurate than a bi-encoder, cheaper than a cross-encoder."],["MRR","Mean reciprocal rank: rewards ranking the first correct result as high as possible."],["nDCG","A ranking metric that rewards putting the most relevant results at the top."]],"m5":[["Pre-training","Training on huge amounts of text to predict the next token. Gives knowledge, not helpful behaviour."],["SFT","Supervised fine-tuning on example prompts and ideal answers, teaching the model to follow instructions."],["RLHF","Reinforcement learning from human feedback: train a reward model on human preferences, then optimise the LLM against it."],["DPO","Direct preference optimisation: learn straight from pairs of better and worse answers, without a separate reward model."],["QLoRA","LoRA fine-tuning on a quantised base model, so large models can be tuned on smaller GPUs."],["Model quantisation","Storing model weights in fewer bits (16 → 8 → 4). Less memory and faster, with a small quality loss."],["Catastrophic forgetting","When fine-tuning on narrow data makes a model worse at the general skills it had before."]],"m6":[["Context window","Everything the model sees in one call: system prompt, tools, history, retrieved documents and its own output."],["Context budget","Treating the context window as limited space to allocate, because more tokens cost more and dilute attention."],["Sliding window memory","Keeping only the last N turns of a conversation in the prompt."],["Conversation summarisation","Replacing older turns with a running summary so long chats fit the window."],["Long-term memory","Facts and past episodes saved to a store and retrieved when relevant to the current task."],["Procedural memory","Learned instructions about how to do things, saved for future runs."]],"m7":[["Context precision","How much of the retrieved context was actually relevant to the question."],["Context recall","Whether the retrieved context contained everything needed to answer."],["Answer relevance","Whether the answer actually addresses the question asked."],["BLEU / ROUGE","Word-overlap scores against a reference answer. Weak for open-ended answers, where good answers can use different words."],["Pointwise vs pairwise evaluation","Pointwise scores one answer on a scale; pairwise picks the better of two, giving a win rate."],["Containment rate","The share of conversations the AI resolves without a human. A key business metric for support bots."],["Confusion matrix","A table of true positives, false positives, true negatives and false negatives for a classifier."]],"m8":[["Guardrails","Checks around a model at three points: input (before it sees text), output (before the user sees it) and action (before a tool acts)."],["PII redaction","Detecting and masking personal data like names, phone numbers and IDs before they reach the model or the logs."],["Groundedness check","Verifying that claims in an answer are supported by the retrieved sources before showing it."],["Tiered guardrails","Running cheap rule-based checks first and expensive model-based checks only when needed, to limit latency and cost."],["Over-strict guardrail","A rule so blunt it blocks useful behaviour. Always pair a refusal with a way forward, like a callback or a human."]],"m9":[["Streaming","Sending tokens to the user as they're generated. Makes responses feel faster without making generation faster."],["SSE (server-sent events)","One-way streaming from server to browser over normal HTTP, with automatic reconnect. The standard for streaming LLM answers."],["WebSockets","A persistent two-way connection where both sides can send at any time. Used for real-time voice and live collaboration."],["Webhook","The server calls your endpoint when a long job finishes, instead of you polling for it."],["RPM and TPM","Requests per minute and tokens per minute: the two rate limits LLM providers apply."],["HTTP 429","'Too many requests': the rate-limit error. Back off with jitter and retry."]],"m11":[["Proxy variable","A feature like postcode that carries the signal of a protected trait even when that trait is removed."],["Demographic parity","Groups receive positive outcomes at similar rates."],["Equal opportunity","Qualified people in each group are approved at similar rates (equal true-positive rates)."],["Equalised odds","Similar true-positive and false-positive rates across groups."],["Fairness definitions conflict","The common fairness definitions can't all be satisfied at once, so choosing one is a policy decision."],["SHAP values","A method that explains an individual prediction by how much each feature pushed it up or down."],["Counterfactual explanation","Explaining a decision by what would have to change: 'approved if income were X higher'."]],"m14":[["MSA vs dialects","Modern Standard Arabic is the formal written form; people speak and type in dialects like Gulf, Levantine, Egyptian and Maghrebi."],["Code-switching","Mixing languages in one message, e.g. Arabic and English, which models and tokenizers handle less reliably."],["Arabizi","Arabic written in Latin letters and numbers, like '7abibi'."],["Arabic normalisation","Standardising text before search: unifying alef forms, ta marbuta and removing diacritics."],["Per-dialect evaluation","Separate golden sets per dialect, labelled by native speakers, because overall scores hide weak dialects."],["Arabic-centric LLMs","Models built with a strong Arabic focus, such as Jais and ALLaM."],["OCR","Optical character recognition: turns images of text into text. Cheap and fast, but loses layout."],["Document AI","Layout-aware extraction that keeps structure such as tables and key-value fields (e.g. Textract, Document Intelligence)."],["Vision-language model (VLM)","A multimodal LLM that reads images directly and can answer questions or extract fields from them."],["Field-level confidence","A confidence score per extracted value, used to route uncertain fields to a human."],["Field-level accuracy","The share of extracted fields that are correct on a labelled set of real documents."],["VAD","Voice activity detection: works out when the user starts and stops speaking."],["ASR","Automatic speech recognition (speech-to-text)."],["TTS","Text-to-speech: turns the agent's reply into audio."],["Cascaded voice pipeline","VAD → ASR → LLM → TTS, each stage streaming to keep total latency around a second."],["Speech-to-speech model","A model that takes audio in and produces audio out directly. Lower latency and keeps tone, but harder to control."],["Barge-in","Letting the user interrupt: the agent detects speech and stops talking."],["Word error rate (WER)","The share of words an ASR system gets wrong. Report it per dialect."]]}};
(function(){var X=window.EXTRAS,M=window.MODULES||[];
X.lessons.forEach(function(e){var m=M.filter(function(x){return x.id===e.mid})[0];if(m&&!m.lessons.some(function(l){return l.id===e.lesson.id}))m.lessons.push(e.lesson);});
if(!M.some(function(m){return m.id===X.module.id}))M.push(X.module);
})();
